What is Ollama?

Ollama is a local LLM runner (like a lightweight inference server).

It lets you run open-source LLMs (e.g., LLaMA 2, Code LLaMA, Mistral, Gemma) on your own machine.

It comes with a CLI + local API (127.0.0.1:11434) for sending prompts and receiving responses.

It’s inference-only: you can’t train models with Ollama, just run them.

 Prerequisites to Install Ollama
Hardware

CPU-only: Works fine, but slower.

GPU (NVIDIA/AMD): Much faster inference.

RAM:

7B models → ~8 GB RAM minimum.

13B models → 16+ GB RAM.

34B models → 32+ GB RAM.

Disk space: Each model is 3–10 GB. Plan for 32 GB+ root volume (as you saw on EC2).

Software

OS: Linux (Ubuntu, Amazon Linux 2023, Debian), macOS, or Windows WSL2.

Packages: curl, git (for install).

Systemd: Ollama installs as a systemd service by default.

⚙️ How Ollama Works (Internals)

Model Storage

Models are stored in /usr/share/ollama/.ollama/models (or ~/.ollama for user installs).

They are pre-converted to Ollama’s GGUF format (optimized for inference).

Service & API

When installed, Ollama runs as a background service:

ollama serve


Exposes API on 127.0.0.1:11434.

You can query it via ollama run <model> "<prompt>" or with HTTP POST requests.

Input Processing

You send a prompt (string).

Example:

ollama run codellama:7b "Write a bash script to check disk usage"


Ollama tokenizes your text into numbers (tokens).

Model Inference

The LLM (e.g., Code LLaMA) runs locally on CPU/GPU.

It predicts the next token repeatedly until it finishes the response.

Uses quantized weights for efficiency (smaller + faster).

Output

Tokens are converted back to text.

Printed to CLI or streamed via API.

You can redirect output into files (> script.sh).

 Input →  Output Flow

You type a prompt (natural language instruction).
Example:

"Write a bash script to monitor CPU usage"


Ollama sends it to the model (Code LLaMA in this case).

Model inference happens locally:

Tokenizes the input.

Predicts next tokens using its pre-trained knowledge.

Builds a script line by line.

Ollama streams the response back to your terminal/API.
Example output:

#!/bin/bash
while true; do
  top -bn1 | head -n 5
  sleep 10
done

 Why It Works

Ollama doesn’t “understand” Linux — it has seen lots of Linux/bash examples in training data (from Code LLaMA, Mistral, etc.).

When you prompt it, it “patterns” similar examples and generates new code/scripts.

summary:

Prereqs → Linux/macOS/WSL2, 8+ GB RAM, enough disk, systemd.

Inputs → Natural-language prompts (ollama run model "...").

Process → Tokenization → Local inference (CPU/GPU) → Generate tokens.

Outputs → Text/script streamed to terminal or API.
